<Project>

  <!--
    THIS FILE DELIBERATELY DOES NOT IMPORT THE ROOT Directory.Build.props (HM-DEC-152).

    MSBuild walks up from a project looking for the first Directory.Build.props and
    stops at it. The usual courtesy is to import the one above before adding to it.
    Not importing it is the whole point here, and the absence of that import is a
    decision rather than an oversight.

    The root file injects Version, and with it AssemblyVersion,
    AssemblyInformationalVersion and BuildStampUtc, into every project in the
    solution. So Ft8Sharp.dll was compiling as a version of Hamlet, carrying
    Hamlet's git commit — and the boundary test cannot see that, because it walks
    assembly references and an injected attribute is not one.

    Ft8Sharp is built to be lifted out of this repository and published on its own,
    under its own MIT LICENSE. An extracted library that announces itself as
    version 1.12.9 of a desktop program it has never heard of is wrong on the first
    line a reader of the package sees. HM-DEC-063 exists so the tree has one answer
    to "what version is this app"; Ft8Sharp is not the app.

    0.1.0 BECAUSE IT HAD TABLES AND NO DECODER.

    A 1.x would claim a maturity this library has not got. The number is the first
    thing anybody sees, and it is the one part of a package that is read by people
    who have read nothing else.

    Bump it when Ft8Sharp gains a capability of its own, not when Hamlet ships.

    0.2.0 ON 2026-09-01, BECAUSE IT GAINED ONE.

    The library now holds the CRC every FT8 message is protected by and the 77-bit
    container every message travels in — the first two pieces of the protocol itself
    it has ever carried, as against tables that described one. Minor rather than
    patch, because it is an addition to what the library can do rather than a
    correction to what it already did. Still not a 1.x: nothing here decodes.

    0.3.0 ON 2026-09-01, BECAUSE IT GAINED ANOTHER.

    The library now turns a message into the 77 bits that go on the air and turns
    77 bits back into the words a person would read — the standard message, free
    text and telemetry, with a dispatcher in front of them that gives every one of
    the protocol's type codes a defined answer. That is the first time anything
    here has produced or consumed a message rather than the envelope one travels
    in, so it is a minor by the same rule. Still not a 1.x: there is no
    demodulator, so nothing here has yet read anything off a radio.

    0.4.0 ON 2026-09-01, BECAUSE IT LEARNED TO REMEMBER.

    FT8 does not spell a long callsign out every time. It sends a hash of one and expects the
    receiver to have heard that station name itself earlier, so a library that cannot remember
    goes deaf at exactly the point a station stops being introduced and starts being referred to.
    This version holds the 22, 12 and 10-bit callsign hashes, the rolling cache that resolves
    them, and the message type that carries one callsign in full and names the other by twelve
    bits alone. A capability the library did not have rather than a correction to one it did, so
    a minor by the same rule as 0.2.0 and 0.3.0.

    It also holds the refusals that go with remembering, and those are the half worth naming: a
    hash this library has never heard resolves to nothing at all, and where two callsigns it HAS
    heard share a hash it returns nothing rather than either of them. Still not a 1.x: there is
    no demodulator, so nothing here has yet read anything off a radio.

    0.5.0 — unit 209. The library can now produce the tones a transmission is made of: a packed
    message becomes the 79 channel symbols an FT8 signal actually sends, the codeword mapped
    through the Gray code and interleaved with the three Costas sync blocks. A capability it did
    not have rather than a correction to one it did, so a minor by the same rule as the three
    before it.

    What that minor does NOT claim, and the distinction matters more here than anywhere so far:
    these symbols have never been held against ft8_lib's own. The reference generator could not be
    built on this machine — there is no C toolchain here — so the sequence is proved against the
    pinned source and against a second implementation in the test project, and against nothing
    else. Step 3's second exit criterion is open. Still not a 1.x, and now for two reasons: there
    is no demodulator, and nothing has yet agreed with anybody but itself.

    0.6.0 — unit 212. THE LIBRARY CAN NOW MAKE THE AUDIO A TRANSMISSION IS.

    Until tonight nothing in this tree turned a symbol into a sample. Ft8Waveform takes the 79
    channel symbols the encoder produces and returns the actual waveform — the tones shaped by a
    Gaussian pulse, the phase running continuously across every symbol boundary, the ends ramped,
    the whole thing laid into a fifteen-second slot, and a second entry point giving the sixteen-bit
    samples a file or a sound card would want. A capability the library did not have rather than a
    correction to one it did, so a minor by the same rule as every one of the five before it.

    And unlike 0.5.0, this one HAS been held against ft8_lib. Every sample of fifty-one messages was
    compared against the WAV upstream's own generator writes for the same message and the largest
    disagreement anywhere was one count in sixteen-bit terms. So what this library produces is not
    merely something we believe is FT8 — it is, sample for sample, the waveform every FT8 decoder in
    the world already reads.

    WHAT THIS MINOR DOES NOT CLAIM, and it is the same shortfall as every version before it.
    NOTHING HERE DECODES. There is no demodulator, no candidate search, no soft symbol and no belief
    propagation. No program has been run against this waveform to see whether it can be read back —
    not ours and not anybody's, because the reference decoder is not built on this machine. The
    evidence is that our samples are upstream's samples, which is a strong statement about the
    synthesizer and no statement at all about reception. Still not a 1.x, and for the reason it has
    always been: this library can now speak and it still cannot hear.

    It also does not claim to be a transmitter and never will be. The synthesizer returns a buffer.
    Nothing in this library opens a sound device, a stream or a port, and nothing plays what it
    makes.

    0.7.0 — THE LIBRARY LOOKS AT A SAMPLE FOR THE FIRST TIME IN ITS LIFE.

    Every version up to 0.6.0 could only speak. This one adds src/Ft8Sharp/Dsp/: a discrete Fourier
    transform written from the mathematics rather than borrowed from anyone, a real-input path, and
    the waterfall spectrogram ft8_lib's own monitor builds — audio in, magnitudes out, at the block
    size, window, oversampling and byte storage read out of the pin. A capability the library did not
    have rather than a correction to one it did, so a minor by the same rule as the six before it.

    The transform is nobody else's code. ft8_lib does not implement one; it vendors KISS FFT, which
    is Mark Borgerding's under BSD-3-Clause, and this library carries one LICENSE and one NOTICE.
    Cooley-Tukey is public literature, so it is written from the decomposition and nothing in that
    folder was read beyond its licence header. There is still no PackageReference here, for anything.

    The evidence: the transform agrees with a naive DFT computed independently in the tests to a
    worst relative error of 4.6e-15 over thirty lengths, measured before any bound was asserted; and
    4424 of 4424 symbols of 56 messages were found again in the spectrum of audio this library
    synthesized itself, with noise alone finding them at 12.4 per cent against a chance rate of 12.5.

    WHAT THIS MINOR DOES NOT CLAIM, and it is the important half.

    NOTHING HERE SEARCHES. Every one of those 4424 symbols was looked for at a frequency and a time
    the caller CHOSE and HANDED IN. There is no Costas correlation, no candidate list, no score and
    no ranking, so this library still cannot find a signal it was not told the location of - which is
    what "hearing" would actually mean.

    AND NOTHING HERE DECODES. No demodulator, no soft symbol, no belief propagation, no message comes
    out the other end. Still not a 1.x. It can speak, and it can now see; it cannot yet hear.

    0.8.0 — IT CAN FIND A TRANSMISSION NOBODY TOLD IT ABOUT.

    Every version up to 0.7.0 had to be handed the answer. The spectrogram in 0.7.0 could show where
    the energy was, but every tone it ever recovered was looked for at a frequency and a moment the
    caller chose, which is not hearing. This one adds Ft8SyncSearch and Ft8Candidate: the Costas sync
    correlation ported from ft8_lib's own ft8_sync_score, and the candidate search across the whole
    passband, ranked in a deterministic total order. It takes a slot of samples and the extents of
    the analysis, AND THERE IS NO PARAMETER THROUGH WHICH IT COULD BE TOLD WHERE TO LOOK — a search
    with a hint cannot be shown not to have used it, so the hint does not exist. A capability the
    library did not have rather than a correction to one it did, so a minor by the same rule as the
    seven before it.

    The evidence: 56 of 56 synthesized messages found at rank 1 out of clean slots and 56 of 56 again
    under noise at a delivered -10.001 dB, at frequencies including one exactly halfway between two
    bins and at offsets on neither the block nor the sub-block grid; 20 of 20 simultaneous
    transmissions found across the passband in one search, with the list 22 deep to cover them; and
    over twenty slots of noise alone the best score was 14 against 31 at a real signal, so no
    noise-only slot ever produced a candidate as strong as a real one. Still no PackageReference
    here, for anything.

    WHAT THIS MINOR DOES NOT CLAIM, and it is again the important half.

    NOTHING HERE DEMODULATES. No soft symbol, no log-likelihood ratio, no belief propagation, no CRC
    check, no message comes out. A CANDIDATE IS A PLACE, NOT A MESSAGE, and a strong sync score is
    not a decode. It says where to point a decoder that does not exist yet. Still not a 1.x.

    And it has never been shown a real signal off a real antenna. Every measurement above is over
    audio this library synthesized itself, which has no fading, no drift and no neighbours.

    0.9.0 — IT CAN BE WRONG AND COME BACK.

    Every version up to 0.8.0 was exact. It packed the right bits, made the right tones, wrote the
    right samples, and found the right place in a slot — and a radio is not exact. The whole reason
    FT8 works at signal levels a human ear cannot hear is that 174 bits carry 91 bits of message and
    the rest is there to repair the damage, and until this version NOTHING IN THIS LIBRARY CORRECTED
    A SINGLE BIT ERROR. src/Ft8Sharp/Ldpc/ held one file and it was the encoder, whose own summary
    says it is not a decoder and cannot become one by accident.

    This version adds LdpcDecoder — ft8_lib's own bp_decode, the belief propagation over the FT8
    LDPC(174,91) code's Tanner graph, ported term for term including the rational tanh and its clamp
    rather than the standard library's, because the arithmetic inside the loop that decides bits is
    part of what is being ported. And it adds Ft8CodewordDecoder, THE GATE: log-likelihood ratios
    in, a message or nothing out, with BOTH parity and the checksum required and neither a partial
    nor a best guess nor a flagged message ever returned. A capability the library did not have
    rather than a correction to one it did, so a minor by the same rule as the eight before it.

    THE EVIDENCE, AND THE SECOND NUMBER MATTERS MORE THAN THE FIRST. Every one of 400 trials
    recovered from 6 bit errors, with recovery falling to zero by 17, measured over 45 error counts
    and 18000 trials rather than taken from a textbook. And across 37952 trials in which a wrong
    message could have come back — damaged codewords, codewords built with the checksum deliberately
    wrong, random ratios, noise with no codeword under it, and a clean codeword with every ratio's
    sign inverted — IT RETURNED A WRONG MESSAGE ZERO TIMES. A decoder that shows an operator a
    callsign nobody sent is worse than one that shows nothing.

    WHAT THIS MINOR DOES NOT CLAIM, and it is again the important half.

    NOTHING HERE EXTRACTS A SYMBOL FROM AUDIO. There is no Gray demapping, no three-bits-per-symbol
    layout, no reading of magnitudes out of a waterfall at a candidate's position, and no
    normalisation of ratios onto upstream's scale. Every ratio this decoder has ever seen was
    constructed from a codeword this library encoded itself. NO CANDIDATE HAS EVER BEEN TURNED INTO
    TEXT AND NO MESSAGE HAS COME OFF A RADIO — not in this version and not in any before it.

    Still not a 1.x. It can speak, it can see, it can find, and it can now repair. The piece that
    turns a place in a waterfall into the numbers this decoder eats does not exist.

    0.9.0 -> 0.10.0 (unit 216). THE LIBRARY GAINS THE CAPABILITY THE WHOLE PHASE IS FOR: IT CAN
    TAKE AUDIO AND RETURN THE TEXT THAT WAS IN IT.

    src/Ft8Sharp/Dsp/ gains Ft8SoftSymbols, which reads 174 log-likelihood ratios out of a waterfall
    at a candidate's position and rescales them to upstream's own variance of 24; and
    Ft8SlotDecoder, which is the whole path — analyse, search, extract, normalise, correct, check
    the checksum, unpack, de-duplicate — samples in, messages out. The pieces above it were all
    proved separately and had never met.

    AND IT WAS JUDGED BY SOMEBODY OTHER THAN ITSELF FOR THE FIRST TIME IN THIS PHASE. Every
    receive-side measurement before tonight was taken against a signal this library synthesized, and
    a port wrong in the same way at both ends passes all of them. This version decodes ft8_lib's own
    off-air recordings against the expected decode lists checked in beside them: 760 OF 1298
    EXPECTED MESSAGES MATCHED ACROSS 60 RECORDINGS, with 23 returned that are not on any list out of
    783 returned.

    WHAT THIS MINOR DOES NOT CLAIM, and it is again the important half.

    NO AUDIO COMES FROM A RADIO. Every sample it has been given came from a file or from its own
    synthesizer; nothing opens a device, a stream or a port. NOTHING IS SCHEDULED TO A UTC SLOT — it
    decodes a buffer somebody hands it and has no idea what time it is. NOTHING REACHES A SCREEN.
    And THE SENSITIVITY IS UNMEASURED: 760 of 1298 is where it stands against those recordings, that
    figure is not comparable with any published threshold, and the measurement that would be has not
    been taken.

    Still not a 1.x — and the reason is now a number rather than a missing piece.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <Version>0.10.0</Version>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    AND NO GIT COMMIT EITHER.

    The SDK appends the repository's source revision to
    AssemblyInformationalVersion by itself, without help from the root file, so
    dropping the inheritance above is not enough on its own: the library would
    still publish "0.1.0+<a Hamlet commit>", which names a repository the extracted
    package has no relationship with. Measured on 2026-08-31 by reading
    obj/Debug/net8.0/Ft8Sharp.AssemblyInfo.cs before and after.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <IncludeSourceRevisionInInformationalVersion>false</IncludeSourceRevisionInInformationalVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
